WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.747 --> 00:00:04.430
♪ The Simpsons ♪

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.890
Narrator: This is the
original title sequence

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.070
to "The Simpsons."

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.920
And this is what it looks like today.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.020
In the older version, Marge's
hair really whips around.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:18.390
But with the revamp, the
motion gets toned down.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.770
Even the paper bag moves less.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.487
Over the last 30-plus years,

00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:25.960
the "Simpsons" animation has
gone through a lot of changes,

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:28.270
with every decade bringing new technology

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.910
that helped the show refine its style.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.880
We talked to two of the main
minds behind "The Simpsons"

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:36.130
who've been with the show since day one

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:39.772
to find out how we got from this to this.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:42.403
Noble spirits, your time has passed.

00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:43.236
[burps]

00:00:44.099 --> 00:00:45.310
Narrator: Here's what
the Simpsons looked like

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:46.820
when they first appeared on TV

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.860
as a recurring segment on
"The Tracey Ullman Show."

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:51.920
If they don't look like the
Simpsons you know today,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.130
that's because these
characters were very much

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.003
still a work in progress.

00:00:56.003 --> 00:00:57.828
Homer: There's nothing to worry about.

00:00:57.828 --> 00:01:00.236
Now everyone go to sleep.

00:01:00.236 --> 00:01:02.230
Narrator: Matt Groening, the
creator of "The Simpsons,"

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.312
would sketch out the one-minute shorts,

00:01:04.312 --> 00:01:07.160
then hand his sketches to
the show's three animators,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.110
who together made up the show's
entire animation department.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:12.837
David Silverman was one of them.

00:01:12.837 --> 00:01:16.040
David: And we sort of would
take Matt's rough drawings

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.940
and cobble a storyboard
between the three of us.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.110
Narrator: The three of them were involved

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.650
in nearly every stage of
the process after that,

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:25.280
from layout, where they draw out

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.610
the characters' key poses or acting,

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.268
to the actual animation,

00:01:29.268 --> 00:01:30.950
to in-betweening, when they create

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:33.520
the intermediate frames
between key frames.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:34.957
To make things more demanding,

00:01:34.957 --> 00:01:37.900
"The Simpsons" was going
for a hand-drawn look,

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.850
so the artists used traditional

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.130
ink-and-paint cel animation.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.570
Starting off with plastic
sheets called cels,

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.790
they would outline images
on the front of the cel

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.550
and paint color on the back.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.650
David estimated that in the early days,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.210
they were working 60- to 80-hour weeks

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.080
to animate just a minute and a half

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.390
between the three of them.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.070
Pressed for time, the animators

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.210
actually just traced over Matt's
drawings in the beginning.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.430
This might be why the
Simpsons in the early shorts

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:05.500
have sharper, more irregular lines,

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.600
in the style that Matt was known for.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.290
They also started out with
a more pronounced version

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:11.565
of the potato-chip lip,

00:02:11.565 --> 00:02:13.006
another signature of Matt's

00:02:13.006 --> 00:02:15.410
straight out of his "Life in Hell" comics.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:17.140
From there, the artists designed

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:19.890
and redesigned the characters
as they went along.

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.660
This led to some pretty
notable inconsistencies,

00:02:22.660 --> 00:02:25.470
like the family's skin color
varying between episodes

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.620
or their proportions changing
from one week to the next.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.600
There's even a 10-second sequence

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.970
where Bart's character design

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.250
shifts noticeably in
three successive shots --

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.960
all within a single scene.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.830
But these quick decisions
also laid the groundwork

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.500
for the defining features
of the "Simpsons" look.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:43.990
Matt said he gave the characters

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.580
four fingers instead of five

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.540
because it'd reduce "pencil mileage,"

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.460
making the figures easier to draw.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.590
And the team painted the
Simpsons yellow for two reasons:

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.620
the color would make them
stand out on TV screens,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.910
and it would suit the characters
who don't have hairlines.

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:00.580
Like showrunner Mike Reiss said,

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.070
the color yellow is
"kinda skin, kinda hair."

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:06.262
Now we get to "The Simpsons"' first season

00:03:06.262 --> 00:03:08.370
as its own TV show.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.660
This is the final episode of season one,

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.160
but it was actually
supposed to be the pilot,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.410
according to Al Jean,
a "Simpsons" showrunner

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.235
who's worked on the show
since its very first season.

00:03:18.235 --> 00:03:19.460
Al: It really didn't look right.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.110
It didn't look like "The Simpsons,"

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.489
so it was actually the last
show aired of the first 13.

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:25.440
In the first season, there
was a learning curve.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.230
We were basically training everybody else

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.327
to draw the characters
as we had conceived them.

00:03:30.327 --> 00:03:31.910
Narrator: First, the animators had to make

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.600
the Simpsons' figures more rounded

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.930
than they were in the shorts

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.740
so that they'd be easier
to turn around in space.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.280
And the designs that made
the characters so iconic

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.190
also made them difficult
to draw consistently.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.950
Artists on the show have described

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:46.420
the "Simpsons" character designs

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.410
as "delicate" and "deceptively simple,"

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.610
because a single stray stroke
could transform a character.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:56.410
Compare Bart to a
character like Bugs Bunny.

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.540
As David said, "Bugs Bunny
has quite a lot of lines,

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:01.480
which makes him easier to capture ...

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.770
there's a greater allowance for mistakes."

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:05.890
But with Bart's hair, for example,

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.290
or his version of the
Simpsons family nose,

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.370
if there's a single line drawn wrong

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:12.210
or with the wrong pencil thickness,

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.790
the character will no
longer look like Bart.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.320
So throughout season one,

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.470
the show developed its model sheets --

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.740
style guides that lay out the right poses,

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:23.930
movements, and expressions
for each character.

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.500
The season also varied a lot

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.670
in how much fluidity is given
to the characters' movements.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:31.120
Fluid animation is when
a character's lines

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.860
flow smoothly, like water.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:34.210
There are points in season one

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:35.930
where the characters are very fluid,

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.700
like Ms. Botz in this scene.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.350
See that head shake she does?

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:41.100
It's almost like the animators drew her

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.760
with more frames per second
than they usually do,

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.750
producing this super-smooth motion

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.040
that stands out next to other scenes.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:50.337
Wacky moments like this are what many fans

00:04:50.337 --> 00:04:52.330
love most about the first season.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.600
But in season two, those wacky moments

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.510
started to become more standardized.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:59.088
Homer: You're living in
a world of make believe,

00:04:59.088 --> 00:05:01.400
with flowers and bells and leprechauns

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.630
and magic frogs with funny little hats!

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.180
Narrator: By then, the show had developed

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.360
an almost 500-page style bible

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.730
laying out exactly how and how not

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.650
to draw the residents of Springfield.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.790
This page lays out various
no-nos for drawing Lisa,

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.300
while another page defines
the eight-point rule

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.630
for her hair, describing
how it should be clustered

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.040
in groups of three, three,
and two on Lisa's head.

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.200
Matt also wanted to
distance "The Simpsons"

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.920
from the house styles of
other animation studios,

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.140
which meant movement was key.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:32.260
In the Disney world,

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.650
characters tend to move in a springy way,

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:35.880
like they're dancing.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.100
And other characters, like
Wile E. Coyote and Bugs Bunny,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:43.020
had bodies that extended and
snapped back like rubber bands.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:45.220
The "Simpsons" team
decided to have characters

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.490
with a more limited range of movements,

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:49.320
with occasional moments of fluidity

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.330
for comedic or dramatic effect.

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.030
They even studied Kermit the Frog,

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.280
because they liked how
he could convey emotion

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.880
with just a slight change
in his Muppet mouth.

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:59.700
This style still left the Simpsons

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.750
plenty of room for expressiveness.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.020
According to Nelson Shin at Akom,

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.630
the South Korean studio that
helps animate "The Simpsons,"

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:09.970
a typical cartoon face maybe
has six different mouths.

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:14.320
A main character on "The
Simpsons" can have as many as 27.

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.220
The backgrounds were another challenge.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.290
Al: If you look at, like,
Moe's in season one,

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:21.158
it really does look like
they're in a 2D environment.

00:06:21.158 --> 00:06:24.180
And it really looks a
lot more 3D subsequently.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.340
David: The artwork was becoming better.

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.600
Just getting perspective
to the backgrounds,

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.070
as opposed to them looking
just sort of, like,

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:31.883
badly drawn. [laughs]

00:06:31.883 --> 00:06:33.300
Narrator: The showrunners also leaned

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:35.550
into other aspects of the "Simpsons" look:

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:38.670
thin outlines and characters
with few sharp edges on them

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.830
and distinctively walled eyes.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:41.900
Those are crucial --

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.550
the big, bulgy, almost circular eyeballs

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:45.860
with black-dot pupils

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.560
that make the Simpsons
look permanently stunned,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.980
like they just got
electrocuted by their TV.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:52.710
The show made a rule that characters' eyes

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.950
had to either be looking
in the same direction

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.500
or turning slightly outward from the nose,

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.230
never cross-eyed like you see
on Saturday morning cartoons,

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:02.600
because that would look too "cute."

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:05.010
Still, the show wasn't
perfectly consistent

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:06.460
on details like these.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.560
For a while, in seasons
three through five,

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.850
some characters' pupils were drawn bigger,

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:13.550
something that David and
Al say was unintentional.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.630
I want my characters to have
more of a confused bird look,

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.150
the sort of like ...

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:19.820
It seemed the jokes played better.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:21.780
And then I think the blank expression,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.580
you as a viewer can just project
whatever you feel about it.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:27.810
Narrator: "The Simpsons" stuck with

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.540
hand-painted cel animation
through the '90s,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.440
but by season seven, the
showrunners were experimenting

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.672
with digital paint in one-off episodes

00:07:35.672 --> 00:07:37.555
like this one, "Radioactive Man,"

00:07:37.555 --> 00:07:39.213
and even digital animation

00:07:39.213 --> 00:07:41.580
in the season seven Halloween special.

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:45.020
In 2002, the show used the
same annual Halloween episode

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.630
to try out digital paint again,

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:49.770
this time for a sequence
where Homer clones himself.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.835
Which actually would have
probably been impossible on cels

00:07:52.835 --> 00:07:55.480
because there are so
many clones and levels.

00:07:55.480 --> 00:07:57.360
There's a certain physical point

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.150
of how many cel levels you can have,

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:00.857
where, of course, digital compositing

00:08:00.857 --> 00:08:03.630
can have infinite number
of levels and characters.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:04.670
Narrator: A few episodes later,

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:07.460
the show switched over to
digital coloring for good.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:09.021
Al: There was just a talk that you had,

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:11.050
when they just said, "You're
gonna have to go digital."

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:12.130
There had been earlier shows

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:14.124
where something wasn't
the right color, maybe,

00:08:14.124 --> 00:08:17.030
but it was impossible to
fix because of the cost,

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:18.870
whereas with digital coloring,

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:20.500
you can fix it faster for cheaper.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.688
And it's a whole new world, it really is.

00:08:22.688 --> 00:08:23.690
Narrator: It also gave the show

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:26.270
more vivid backgrounds
and crisper graphics

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:28.330
and, at first, heavier black borders

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:30.210
around characters and objects,

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.495
an accident that the show fixed later on.

00:08:32.495 --> 00:08:33.810
We would have a problem where

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.209
you had the camera move in and out,

00:08:35.209 --> 00:08:38.080
the line would get thinner and
thicker, which we all hated.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:39.425
David: We were able to figure out

00:08:39.425 --> 00:08:40.950
that you could basically dial in

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.360
the size of the lines and so forth.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.540
So as the seasons progressed,

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:47.180
pretty quickly we just got into the more

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:49.400
technical remedies for these situations.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.100
Narrator: And it's these
experiments with digital

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:52.820
that set the stage for "The Simpsons"'

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:55.187
biggest transformation yet:

00:08:55.187 --> 00:08:56.670
"The Simpsons Movie."

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:59.690
Trailer: The greatest
Simpsons family adventure

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.410
of all time!

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:02.857
Narrator: For a while,
the showrunners thought

00:09:02.857 --> 00:09:06.020
"The Simpsons Movie" would
be fully CG-animated,

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:09.277
but they didn't end up going
the full-CG route for the film.

00:09:09.277 --> 00:09:10.712
Moe: "The Simpsons Movie" ...

00:09:10.712 --> 00:09:11.570
[splat]

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:13.113
in 2D!

00:09:13.113 --> 00:09:14.600
Narrator: Instead, the team mixed

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.660
traditional 2D animation with 3D CGI

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:19.570
to make the show's classic style work

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:22.840
in the most wide-screen
movie format, CinemaScope.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.220
Since Springfield would appear

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:25.820
on a 100-foot movie screen,

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:28.480
the color-design team used
a larger color palette

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:31.340
for the movie than they'd
ever used on the TV show.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:32.818
And while backgrounds on the show

00:09:32.818 --> 00:09:35.900
were often flat-colored
and lit by a single source,

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:39.230
the movie called for backgrounds
that were richer in detail.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.430
To help focus the audience's eyes,

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:44.360
the artists added shadow
vignettes to the backgrounds

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:46.630
and put a tone shadow and drop shadow

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:48.610
on characters in every scene.

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:51.270
On TV, they'd only given
characters those shadows

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:52.890
for scenes that were set at night

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:55.330
or supposed to be especially dramatic.

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:57.500
The more nuanced lighting
and shadows in the movie

00:09:57.500 --> 00:09:59.180
created depth in every shot,

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:02.050
so that certain characters
and objects pop out at you.

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:05.377
These artistic elevations stuck
around long after the film.

00:10:05.377 --> 00:10:07.110
"The Simpsons" started incorporating

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.518
more computer animation and 3D effects,

00:10:09.518 --> 00:10:11.950
especially for complicated backgrounds.

00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:14.970
The showrunners even created
a special scene-planning team

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.330
to digitally animate elaborate sequences.

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:19.270
David: The scene planners
helped in our camera moves

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:21.490
and making them more sort of cinematic.

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:25.440
We would try to emulate the
idea of a multiplane camera,

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.255
where you have planes of animation

00:10:27.255 --> 00:10:29.360
so that it feels like the
camera's physically moving

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:31.347
as opposed to just zooming in and out.

00:10:31.347 --> 00:10:33.890
Narrator: And working in
widescreen HD animation

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.700
for the movie proved to
be a useful trial run

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:40.100
for two years later, when
the show, in its 20th season,

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:42.073
officially moved to HD.

00:10:42.073 --> 00:10:43.757
Isn't it about time you went digital?

00:10:43.757 --> 00:10:45.490
Watch your mouth, you little smart-ass!

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:49.100
Al: HD was a whole other enormous change

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.330
that we had no money and
no time to prepare for.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.690
Narrator: Higher digital resolution

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:55.990
left less room for mistakes or shortcuts.

00:10:55.990 --> 00:10:57.860
Al: You really have to
be much more careful

00:10:57.860 --> 00:11:00.117
that the backgrounds don't
look shoddy in one spot

00:11:00.117 --> 00:11:01.770
and much better in another.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.320
There's so much information in the frame.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:05.978
Where we used to get by with, you know,

00:11:05.978 --> 00:11:07.680
you'd have a little piece
of paper on a bulletin board

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.760
and a scribble on it,
it looks terrible now.

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.300
Narrator: And in the first HD seasons,

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.665
artists couldn't save
time by recycling shots

00:11:14.665 --> 00:11:16.940
from past episodes like they normally do.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:19.037
Al: In season 20, it was really tricky.

00:11:19.037 --> 00:11:21.150
There was nothing that we could rely on

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:22.710
from past shows to fill in.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:26.970
We had to restart our whole
library of backgrounds,

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:28.910
our whole library of old shots.

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.713
So we've had to go redo things

00:11:30.713 --> 00:11:33.890
like the comic-book
store or the town square,

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.293
totally rethinking how
they would look in HD.

00:11:36.293 --> 00:11:38.260
Narrator: And the HD
format also took the show

00:11:38.260 --> 00:11:42.710
from its traditional 4:3
aspect ratio to 16:9.

00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:44.940
With this switch, Matt
said the "Simpsons" team

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:46.760
had to "rethink everything."

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.020
David: So we became even more conscious

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:52.360
of thinking about the space cinematically.

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:53.400
We talked more about that.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.520
"OK, we're gonna have
a flat-space shot here

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:56.480
for this part of it.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:57.940
Now let's go to a deep-space shot."

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:01.860
So, you could take more
advantage of a longer shot.

00:12:01.860 --> 00:12:03.950
You could play more of
the action in a wide shot.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:05.690
Narrator: The wider frame forced the team

00:12:05.690 --> 00:12:07.970
to think more about shot composition.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:10.340
This meant creating
more asymmetry in shots.

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:12.680
Al: Where you have a little
piece of garbage here

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:14.520
or a fire extinguisher on the wall there,

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:17.670
or several things that
just are like real life

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:20.650
rather than just these
perfect rectangles everywhere.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:22.950
Narrator: The show's iconic
opening was also redone

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:24.940
for the first time since season two.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.320
This HD makeover was
somewhat controversial

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:28.390
in the eyes of fans,

00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:29.900
who said characters like Marge

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.930
looked too rigid in their movement.

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:33.630
Al: The movements aren't
quite as fluid, yeah.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.090
And what happened there was we said,

00:12:36.090 --> 00:12:37.890
well, we've gotta go to HD
and we need a new main title,

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.219
which is fairly lengthy, and they said,

00:12:39.219 --> 00:12:41.527
"You have no money." [laughs]

00:12:41.527 --> 00:12:45.380
We had to figure out ways to
get it done without extra time,

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:48.210
without extra money,
and put in new things.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.260
Narrator: The artists were
also still getting used

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:51.462
to animating in HD.

00:12:51.462 --> 00:12:53.500
David: I think everybody
just got used to using it

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:54.680
more organically.

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:56.360
So at the beginning it
was a little stiffer,

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.720
and we try to be conscious
of that and try to make sure

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:01.060
that the characters are
moving more fluidly.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:03.140
Narrator: Even in recent seasons,

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:05.370
characters seem to have bouncier movement,

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:09.830
straying away from the stiffness
of some early HD episodes.

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:11.270
The show's still figuring out

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.313
some other aspects of its look, too,

00:13:13.313 --> 00:13:15.310
like how much shadow to use.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:16.597
David: Sometimes we go overboard,

00:13:16.597 --> 00:13:19.550
and I think there's
too much use of shadow.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:21.007
We sort of try to balance it out.

00:13:21.007 --> 00:13:22.180
Narrator: And how much color.

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:25.038
David: We still follow
our basic 200 colors,

00:13:25.038 --> 00:13:26.910
approximately, to paint things,

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:29.360
but we vary the tones of them.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:31.280
We try to keep a sensibility

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.930
that's connected to the
original color palette.

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:36.780
Narrator: Lately, the
show's also revitalized

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:39.350
some of its sets,
including adding new jokes

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:40.560
into the town square.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.450
And the artists have polished
up old character models,

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:46.320
especially when dusting off
characters from earlier seasons,

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.670
like Comic Book Guy, who
they had to scale down

00:13:48.670 --> 00:13:50.230
from his season-two model,

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:53.350
which means "The Simpsons" is
still tinkering with its look

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:55.480
after more than three decades.

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:57.920
And it's this constant
tweaking and adapting

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.200
that's helped the show last

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.170
through so many different eras of TV.

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:03.700
That's what helped make "The Simpsons"

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:06.160
the longest-running
scripted primetime series,

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:08.300
the longest-running American sitcom,

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:12.633
and arguably the most
successful animated show ever.

